1339 0 obj
  << /ActualText (A different approach\054 which I will call Orbit interpretation\054 views the apparent \034branching timeline\035 as a feature of a single causal loop over sufficiently high steps of iteration from the initial conditions\056 \050Zoom out\056\051 This is applicable even if the previous assumption of time decoupled from the chain of causality does not prove to be a valid premise\056 If we instead frame our system through the lens of a cyclical departure and return to the same initial conditions \050think expansion and contraction of the universe\051\054 then any \034rewind\035 could be instead achieved by a sufficiently high iteration of step c along t\054 in the quantity of one universal cycle minus the desired rewind amount\056\n\nThe nature of both interpretations comes with the caveat that the traveler never returns to a point of exactly the same spatial conditions \050even when the differences appear so negligible as to be nonexistent\051\054 only approximately the same point\056 Such is the nature of complex dynamical systems\056 Notably this means that the apparent circular cycle of time is more accurately modeled as a spiral\054 one of the core visual motifs of Life is Strange\056\n\nThese philosophy of time travel negate any logical paradox or inconsistency\054 because any such perturbations by the time traveler do not actually violate the causal sequence\056)
     /K [ 109 ]
     /P 1340 0 R
     /Pg 1230 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

